.TH PIDLY 1 "" "pidly" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pidly \- a ticket tracker that is just files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pidly
.I command
[\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Work lives in a
.I .pidly
directory as plain files. A ticket is a directory holding an
.I info
file, and its state is its location: a closed ticket sits in its container's
.I c
directory, so nothing records a status that could disagree with the files.
.PP
Every command is something a person could do with
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR mv (1)
and an editor, leaving the repository byte-identical. The exception is
claiming, which needs a lock to be safe while other agents are running. A
checkout where pidly was never installed remains fully usable by hand, and
.I .pidly/README.md
gives the equivalent of every command below.
.SH COMMANDS
.nf
  init           create .pidly here, or refresh what it generates
  new [-p N] [-n "3 8"] [-f name=value] <title>
                 create a ticket, optionally under parent N, optionally
                 blocked on other tickets; -f sets a field, and every field
                 the schema requires needs one; body is read from stdin
  claim <n>      take ticket n
  release [-m note] <n>
                 give it back, optionally recording where you got to
  status <n>     who holds it, and whether that can be verified
  reap [n]       clear claims whose process is positively gone, one ticket or
                 the whole tracker; never a claim it cannot check
  edit [-a|-b|-r OLD -w NEW] [-f] <n>
                 open it in $EDITOR; -b replaces the body from stdin, -a
                 appends to it, and -r OLD -w NEW replaces one passage of it
                 literally, refusing text that is absent or appears twice. -b
                 refuses a body that lost more than half of what it replaced;
                 -f says you meant it
  set <n> <field> [value]
                 write a project field; with no value, clear it. Also title
                 and needs, which are the format's lines rather than the
                 schema's: a needs on a ticket that does not exist is refused
  needs [-a <n>] [-d <n>] <number>
                 what it needs, one a line; -a adds one and -d drops one,
                 without retyping the others
  fields         give every ticket a slot for every field the schema declares,
                 adding keys only \(em never removing, reordering or restamping
  close <n>      finish it: move it into its container's c/
  reopen <n>     move it back, reopening any closed ancestors
  reparent [-r] <n> <parent>
                 move a ticket under another one, or -r to the top. Never
                 changes whether it is closed, and refuses a destination that
                 would
  list [-a] [--stale 7d]
                 takeable work \(em unclaimed, unblocked and not deferred; -a
                 shows every open ticket with its state; --stale shows open
                 work untouched for that long, oldest first
  field [-a] <name>
                 that field's value in every open ticket, one row each, - for
                 a ticket that has no value; -a includes closed work
  show [-p|-F|-b|-f <name>] <n>
                 one ticket: the whole info file, or -p its directory, -F its
                 field block, -b its body, -f one field's value
  tree           the hierarchy, closed tickets marked [x]
  check          report anything wrong; changes nothing, nonzero if it finds
                 something, so it can run in CI. Notes \(em drift a migration
                 would close \(em are printed without failing it
  agents [-f F]  write .pidly/README.md and a summary into AGENTS.md (or F),
                 replacing the summary if already present
  claude         write the Claude Code integration into .claude: a skill, the
                 slash commands, and a permissions block for the read-only
                 commands
  man            the manual page, in roff, on stdout: pidly man > docs/pidly.1
  --version      the version of pidly this is
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.nf
  PIDLY_PID      the agent's process (default: $CLAUDE_PID, else $PPID).
                 Recorded with its start time, so a recycled pid is never
                 mistaken for it.
  PIDLY_AGENT    an agent with no local process \(em a cloud session, a CI job.
                 Requires PIDLY_LEASE: nothing else can show it is alive.
  PIDLY_LEASE    how long the claim is good for: 90s, 30m, 2h, 3d.
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .pidly/
The tracker. One directory per ticket, numbered, nested by parentage.
.TP
.I .pidly/schema
The project's fields, one stanza each.
.TP
.I .pidly/README.md
The format, generated by
.BR "pidly agents" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B check
exits non-zero when it finds something wrong, so it can run in CI. Everything
else exits non-zero on refusal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR mv (1),
.BR find (1)
